 stay close to it while optimizing a reward. Reward fine-tuning of masked diffusion models. To handle intractable sequence likelihoods, d1 and wd1 use denoising likelihood approximations ( Zhao et al., 2025 ; Tang et al., 2026 ) , while SPG uses lower and upper evidence bounds ( Wang et al., 2026a ) . Beyond reward fine-tuning, TUBE provides a further upper bound on the evidence of masked diffusion models ( Ivanov et al., 2026 ) . Alternatively, AGRPO treats denoising as an MDP with tractable stepwise action probabilities ( Zhan, 2026 ) , and TraceRL updates policies along recorded inference trajectories ( Wang et al., 2026b ) . DMPO and Discrete Tilt Matching learn reward-tilted distributions ( Zhu et al., 2026c ; Chen et al., 2026 ) . DRAKES instead uses differentiable relaxations for biological sequence design when reward gradients are available ( Wang et al., 2025a ) . Joint distillation and fine-tuning. In continuous diffusion, Diff-Instruct++ regularizes one-step reward optimization with an integral KL ( Luo, 2025 ) , DMDR combines distribution matching with reinforcement learning ( Jiang et al., 2025 ) , and RTDMD uses reward-tilted distribution matching for few-step flow generators ( Huang et al., 2026 ) . For masked image diffusion, Soft-Di[M]O refines a one-step generator through soft embeddings. Its adversarial refinement uses real images, and its reward fine-tuning requires differentiable rewards ( Zhu et al., 2026b ) . IDRF (ours) uses inverse distillation as the regularizer of trajectory-based policy gradients, supports reward fine-tuning directly in the few-step regime, and requires neither pretraining data nor reward gradients. 5 Experiments We evaluate IDRF on regulatory-DNA design, CLIP-guided image generation, and sentiment steering of review text. Across these tasks, we study its ability to achieve high reward with few sampling steps and preserve sample quality through inverse-distillation regularization. 5.1 Regulatory-DNA design Setup. Followin