 assisted with language editing, code implementation, computational checks, and mathematical analysis during preparation. The authors are responsible for the claims, results, and final manuscript. Ethics statement This work develops a method for reward fine-tuning of masked diffusion models and involves no human subjects. All experiments use public datasets and pretrained models: the enhancer activity data of Gosai et al. (2024) , the ImageNet-trained MaskGIT ( Chang et al., 2022 ) , and the Amazon Reviews 2023 corpus ( Hou et al., 2026 ) . We do not redistribute review text. The DNA sequences are scored only by predictive models and were not synthesized or tested experimentally. Reward fine-tuning is dual-use. In particular, steering text toward a target sentiment could be misused to produce deceptive reviews at scale, and our sentiment experiment serves only as a benchmark for reward hacking. Reproducibility statement Appendix A gives complete proofs of the theoretical results. Appendices C , D and E list the models, data processing, hyperparameters, sampling settings, evaluation met
